\title{Using the header from another package}
\description{
The labelling that \code{\link[=polymer]{polymer()}} uses to find which input features cover
each triangle is a dependency-free, header-only C++17 file,
\code{polymer/overlay.hpp} (MIT). Another package can use it directly, for
example to label a mesh of its own from a constrained triangulation with
its \code{edge_sources}, without going through R.
}
\section{Setting up}{

In the other package's \code{DESCRIPTION}, link to polymer (and to 'cpp11' if
that is how it talks to R; the header itself needs only the C++ standard
library):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{LinkingTo: cpp11, polymer
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The header needs C++17, which is not the default in every R version that
polymer supports, so set it in \code{src/Makevars} (and \code{src/Makevars.win}):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{CXX_STD = CXX17
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Then \verb{#include <polymer/overlay.hpp>}. Everything is in the
\code{polymer::overlay} namespace.
}

\section{What it takes}{

\verb{polymer::overlay::label()} takes borrowed, 0-based views of
\itemize{
\item a \code{Mesh}: counter-clockwise triangles (\code{tri}, three vertex ids each)
and the constraint edges (\code{edge}, two vertex ids each);
\item \code{EdgeSources}: one row for each constraint edge and input segment
lying on it, with the segment's \code{feature} and whether it runs the same
way as the edge (\code{forward}), as from \code{cdtr::cdt_triangulate(edge_sources = TRUE)};
\item a \code{FillRule}: \code{NonZero}, \code{EvenOdd} or \code{Positive}.
}

It returns \code{Labels}: \code{tri_set}, the covering set of each triangle (\code{-1}
when uncovered), the sets in compressed form (\code{set_offset},
\code{set_feature}), and \code{inconsistent_edges}, a self-check count where a
non-zero value means the noding is broken. No coordinates are used. The
header's own comments give the details.
}

\section{A minimal example}{

One counter-clockwise triangle whose three edges belong to feature 0, all
running the same way as the edge:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{#include <cpp11.hpp>
#include <polymer/overlay.hpp>

[[cpp11::register]]
cpp11::writable::integers label_one() \{
  using namespace polymer::overlay;
  std::vector<int32_t> tri = \{0, 1, 2\};
  std::vector<int32_t> edge = \{0, 1,  1, 2,  2, 0\};
  std::vector<int32_t> es_edge = \{0, 1, 2\};
  std::vector<int32_t> es_feature = \{0, 0, 0\};
  std::vector<int8_t> es_forward = \{1, 1, 1\};

  Mesh m;
  m.tri = tri.data();   m.n_tri = 1;
  m.edge = edge.data(); m.n_edge = 3;
  EdgeSources s;
  s.edge = es_edge.data();       s.feature = es_feature.data();
  s.forward = es_forward.data(); s.n = 3;

  Labels l = label(m, s, FillRule::NonZero);
  // l.tri_set is \{0\}: the triangle is covered by set 0, whose only
  // feature is 0; l.inconsistent_edges is 0
  return cpp11::writable::integers(l.tri_set.begin(), l.tri_set.end());
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{label()} throws \code{std::exception} on malformed input, so catch it at the
R boundary (cpp11 does this for exceptions that escape a registered
function).
}

